text:
Watch me: unpack a business question
For “Should stores introduce manager-retention incentives to improve profit?” identify:

outcome and candidate explanatory variables;
variable types and observational unit;
descriptive and relationship methods;
whether causal language is justified; and
information needed before a decision.

Now you try: data-quality brief





Create a compact audit containing:

observational unit and target population;
roles and types for five variables;
two useful descriptive statistics for Profit;
one appropriate visualisation; and
two data-quality or scope limitations.

Now you try: prioritise relationships





From the correlation matrix:

identify the strongest positive and negative relationships;
create two scatterplots;
flag one possible multicollinearity concern;
identify one weak relationship that may still matter in a multiple model; and
write two cautious business interpretations.

Now you try: decision model





Develop a reduced profit model that supports the incentive question.
Your output must include:

fitted equation and variable coding;
three coefficient interpretations;
coefficient and overall tests;
adjusted R^2 and diagnostics;
a prediction for one observed store; and
a comparison with its actual profit.

ETF case implications
Possible evidence may suggest that tenure and skill are positively associated with performance, but:

tenure develops over time;
incentives may affect retention rather than tenure directly;
omitted store characteristics may confound relationships;
current data are observational; and
an incentive programme has costs not represented in the regression.

Now you try: executive recommendation





Write a five-sentence executive recommendation:

decision and strength of recommendation;
most relevant quantitative evidence;
practical meaning;
principal limitation; and
a pilot or additional evidence that would reduce uncertainty.

Watch me: decode an exam question
Underline:

the population or decision target;
variable types and units;
claim direction;
supplied sample information;
requested method and output; and
required contextual conclusion.

Then sketch the workflow before calculating.

Now you try: rapid method map





For eight instructor-provided mini-scenarios, including a categorical independence test, record only:

parameter, outcome, or objective;
appropriate method;
key assumption or constraint;
Excel tool or function; and
conclusion template.

Compare answers before doing any arithmetic.

Now you try: reconstruct the model





From your Excel output:

write the fitted equation;
interpret two slopes;
identify any meaningless intercept interpretation;
predict price for one observed property; and
calculate its residual.

Watch me: coefficient evidence
For each coefficient:

identify estimate and standard error;
verify t=\hat\beta_j/SE(\hat\beta_j);
compare p-value with \alpha;
inspect the confidence interval; and
state the conditional business conclusion.

Now you try: decide what contributes





At \alpha=0.05:

identify predictors with evidence of contribution;
identify candidates for removal;
explain why p-value alone is not enough to delete a variable; and
name one variable that business logic might require even if its p-value is large.

Now you try: compare two models





Fit a full and a reduced real-estate model.
Compare:

adjusted R^2;
standard error of regression;
coefficient stability;
residual behaviour; and
interpretability.

Recommend one model and justify the trade-off.

Now you try: interpret the ANOVA block





Using your model output:

state the F-test hypotheses;
report Significance F;
make the decision at 5%;
write the contextual conclusion; and
explain why it does not prove every slope is non-zero.

Now you try: underwriting model





Fit the underwriting model and:

interpret the proficiency slope;
interpret both method coefficients;
predict the score for proficiency 100 under each method;
test each coefficient at 5%; and
interpret adjusted R^2 and Significance F.

Model reduction is an argument
A defensible reduction process considers:

the business question;
coefficient evidence and intervals;
adjusted R^2 and predictive error;
coefficient stability after refitting;
hierarchy and important controls; and
residual diagnostics.

Do not repeatedly delete the largest p-value without explanation.

Residual diagnostics
Look for:

curvature: missing nonlinear structure;
funnel shape: non-constant variance;
clusters: missing groups;
extreme residuals: poor fit for particular observations; and
influential points: observations that may strongly affect coefficients.

Watch me: refit and compare
Remove one defensible candidate from the full real-estate model, refit, and compare:

remaining coefficient estimates;
adjusted R^2;
standard error;
residual pattern; and
clarity of interpretation.

Record the reason for the change before viewing whether fit “improves”.

Now you try: model recommendation





Recommend either the full or reduced model.
Your brief must include:

fitted equation;
two coefficient interpretations;
overall and individual evidence;
adjusted R^2 and residual assessment;
one prediction; and
two limitations.

Learning outcomes
On successful completion of this unit, you should be able to:

classify different data types and apply appropriate visualisation tools for effective data presentation;
apply probability calculations and interpretations in a given business problem;
apply random sampling concepts to interpret statistics calculated from business data and conduct hypothesis testing;
analyse relationships between business data using appropriate tools;
build simple and multiple regression models in Excel and communicate the results in relevant business contexts; and
formulate and solve basic business-modelling problems in Excel.

What will we learn?
Across the eight weeks, you will learn to:

classify and visualise business data;
calculate and interpret probabilities;
reason from samples and test business claims;
analyse relationships between variables;
build simple and multiple regression models; and
formulate and solve business modelling problems in Excel.

Now you try: age and market price





On HouseSize:

plot market price against house age;
add a linear trendline;
calculate correlation;
interpret its sign and magnitude; and
identify whether the plot supports a linear summary.

Watch me: regression in Excel
For Spend as Y and Income as X:

Data → Data Analysis → Regression;
select the labelled Y and X ranges;
tick Labels and request residual output;
locate coefficients, R^2, standard errors, and p-values; and
write the fitted equation.

Now you try: interpret IncomeSpend





From the Excel output:

report and interpret the intercept;
report and interpret the slope;
predict the spending change for an AUD 10,000 income increase;
state whether the intercept is practically meaningful; and
compare the slope sign with the correlation sign.

Now you try: model audit





Audit the IncomeSpend model:

inspect its residual plot;
identify one strength and one limitation;
assess whether the line is suitable for prediction;
name one omitted variable; and
state whether a causal interpretation is justified.

Now you try: prediction guardrails





Before predicting spending for a household income of AUD 500,000:

check the observed income range;
label the request interpolation or extrapolation;
explain the main risk;
recommend an alternative if the request is unsupported.

Watch me: a defensible regression brief
A concise brief contains:

business question and variable roles;
scatterplot and correlation;
fitted equation with coefficient interpretations;
R^2, slope test, and prediction;
residual and range checks; and
a recommendation with limitations.

Group brief: recommend a launch price
Prepare a one-page pricing brief for the manager using your workbook. Include:

the ToolPak equation and one output check;
a labelled formula model and best listed feasible price;
one baseline alternative and both sensitivity results; and
a recommendation, one limitation, and a revisit condition.
